# Everything in this repo is run on Linux — inside the Proxmox container, or on
# the Proxmox host. A CRLF line ending in a shell script is not cosmetic there:
# the shebang becomes "/usr/bin/env bash\r", which the kernel tries to execute
# verbatim and fails with a bad-interpreter error. Cloning on Windows with
# core.autocrlf=true would do exactly that, so the endings are pinned here
# rather than left to whatever the cloner's global config happens to say.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Belt and braces for the files where a CR is actually fatal.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.c         text eol=lf
*.h         text eol=lf
Makefile    text eol=lf
*.service   text eol=lf
tools/*     text eol=lf
VERSION     text eol=lf

# Exception, and it has to come last so it wins over the *.c / *.h rules above:
# game/world/ is a verbatim copy of the client's source/world/ (see game/Makefile's
# header), and game/Makefile's drift guard compares the two trees with `cmp -s` —
# bytes, not lines. The client repo pins `* -text`, so source/world/block.h is stored
# with CRLF endings; normalising the copy here to LF would make `cmp -s` disagree on
# every byte of every line ending the moment either repo is freshly checked out, and
# the guard would fail a build that has no actual drift in it. Storing these verbatim
# is what keeps the guard measuring the thing it is meant to measure. Nothing in this
# directory is executed as a script, so no shebang can be broken by a CR.
game/world/** -text
